    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the number of days \\( d \\) for which the colony of bacteria exceeds 100, we start by understanding its growth pattern. The number of bacteria doubles each day, with an initial count of 3 bacteria.\n\nLet \\( N \\) be the number of bacteria after \\( d \\) days. The relationship can be expressed by the recurrence relation:\n\n\\[\nN_{d+1} = 2 \\times N_d\n\\]\n\nStarting with \\( N_0 = 3 \\),\n\nwe compute the number of bacteria, \\( N \\), at the end of each day iteratively:\n\n\\[\nN_1 = 2 \\times N_0 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n\\]\n\n\\[\nN_2 = 2 \\times N_1 = 2 \\times 6 = 12\n\\]\n\n\\[\nN_3 = 2 \\times N_2 = 2 \\times 12 = 24\n\\]\n\n\\[\nN_4 = 2 \\times N_3 = 2 \\times 24 = 48\n\\]\n\n\\[\nN_5 = 2 \\times N_4 = 2 \\times 48 = 96\n\\]\n\nSince \\( N_5 = 96 \\) is less than 100 and \\( N_6 = 2 \\times 96 = 192 \\) is greater than 100, we can conclude that the colony will have more than 100 bacteria on the 6th day.\n\nTherefore, the first day the colony has more than 100 bacteria is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
