      "text": "Let's reason this out:\n\nThe colony has the following quantities: \n\n\\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|} \\hline Day & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6 & 7 \\\\ \\hline Quantity of bacteria & 3 & 6 & 12 & 24 & 48 & 96 & 192 \\\\ \\hline \\end{tabular}\n\nThe number of bacteria doubles every day, that gives us the first answer: 7.\n\nWe can also use another way, although we just need to find the right number. To do that, we can think:\n\nLet the first day which ends with the colony having more than 100 bacteria be \"x\" days after the beginning.\n\nThen the number of bacteria on the \"x\" day would be:\n\n3 times 2 to the power of \"x\" > 100\n\n2 to the power of \"x\" > 100/3\n\nTake the base 2 logs of both sides:\n\nx > log 100/3 / log 2\n\nx > 2.6309\n\nThe first answer is 7.\n\nAnswer: 7\n\n\\boxed{7}",
